   208	## 3. THEOREM T2 — the `E3` normal form and its three laws
   209	
   210	`E3` is the top even equation and the analogue of `O2`. Collecting it the same
   211	way — split `D1 = D2' + F`, `E1 = E2' + G` with `F = d eta G`, substitute
   212	`E2 = bZeta^2`, `D2 = aZeta^3`, `C2 = dZ eta s` — produces massive cancellation.
   213	
   214	**`G`-free part.** Every surviving monomial carries `Z^2`:
   215	
   216	```text
   217	E3|_{G=0} = Z^2 [ 12ab eta^3 eta'(eta' + 2Z eta'')
   218	                 + 4d s(eta s' - eta' s)
   219	                 + 8b eta(q eta' - q' eta)
   220	                 + 12a eta^3 r' ].
   221	```
   222	
   223	(The four groups come respectively from `6D2'E2''-4D2''E2'`-type terms, from the
   224	`C1^{(0)},C2,s` block, from the `q` block, and from `12Z D2 r'`. In the first
   225	group the coefficients of `Z eta^4 eta'`, `Z eta^4 eta''` and `Z^3 eta^2 eta'^3`
   226	all cancel identically; only `12Z^2 eta^3 eta'^2 + 24Z^3 eta^3 eta' eta''`
   227	survives. The spec's `e=0` corner display `4Z^2 I = 0` with
   228	`I = 3A0 r' - 2B q' + t s s'` (`spec:220-226`) is the `eta' = 0` specialization,
   229	which I used as a control.)
   230	
   231	**`G`-part.** The `G`-linear terms again collapse:
   232	
   233	```text
   234	E3 - E3|_{G=0}
   235	 = 12a Z^2 eta[(eta eta'' - eta'^2)G + eta eta' G']
   236	   - (3a/b)[ eta G^2 + 2Z eta' G^2 + 2Z eta G G' ],
   237	```
   238	
   239	and the bracket in the last line is exactly `(Z eta^2 G^2)'/eta`. Hence:
   240	
   241	> **THEOREM T2.** On the live section, `E3` is equivalent to
   242	>
   243	> ```text
   244	> Z^2 * Xi = (3a/b) * (Z eta^2 G^2)' / eta,                            (3.1)
   245	> ```
   246	>
   247	> equivalently `eta Z^2 Xi = (3a/b)(Z eta^2 G^2)'`, where
   248	>
   249	> ```text
   250	> Xi = 12ab eta^3 eta'(eta'+2Z eta'')            (i)
   251	>    +  4d s(eta s' - eta' s)                    (ii)
   252	>    +  8b eta(q eta' - q' eta)                  (iii)
   253	>    + 12a eta^3 r'                              (iv)
   254	>    + 12a eta[(eta eta'' - eta'^2)G + eta eta' G'].   (v)
   255	> ```
   256	
   257	Since `a,b,eta,G` are nonzero the right side of (3.1) is nonzero, so `Xi != 0`.
   258	Three laws follow.
   259	
   260	**(L1) Degree law.** `deg[(Z eta^2 G^2)'/eta] = e + 2g` with leader
   261	`(3a/b)(1+2e+2g) eta_e G_g^2`. Hence
   262	
   263	```text
   264	deg Xi = e + 2g - 2,   leader(Xi) = (3a/b)(1+2e+2g) eta_e G_g^2.       (3.2)
   265	```
   266	
   267	The five families have exact degrees and leaders
   268	
   269	| family | degree | leader | present iff |
   270	|---|---:|---|---|
   271	| (i) | `5e-2` | `12ab e^2(2e-1) eta_e^5` | `e>=1` |
   272	| (ii) | `2sigma+e-1` | `4d(sigma-e) eta_e s_sigma^2` | `s!=0, sigma!=e` |
   273	| (iii) | `2e+m-1` | `8b(e-m) eta_e^2 q_m` | `q!=0, m!=e` |
   274	| (iv) | `3e+n-1` | `12a n eta_e^3 r_n` | `r'!=0` |
   275	| (v) | `3e+g-2` | `12a e(g-1) eta_e^3 G_g` | `e>=1, g!=1` |
   276	
   277	Families (i) and (v) exceed, equal, or fall below the target `e+2g-2` according
   278	as `g < 2e`, `g = 2e`, `g > 2e` — the same trichotomy for both. Family (ii) has
   279	degree `= e+1 mod 2` while the target has degree `= e mod 2`: **(ii) can never
   280	match the target and can never cancel against (i)**, whose degree is also
   281	`= e mod 2`. This is the parity separation the spec used at `spec:255-262`, here
   282	derived for the live section.
   283	
   284	**(L2) Order law.** By degree/order duality applied to (3.1),
   285	`ord[(Z eta^2 G^2)'/eta] = epsilon + 2nu` with lowest coefficient
   286	`(3a/b)(1+2epsilon+2nu) eta~(0) G~(0)^2 != 0`, so
   287	
   288	```text
   289	ord Xi = epsilon + 2 nu - 2,   hence   epsilon + 2 nu >= 2.            (3.3)
   290	```
   291	
   292	Evaluating (3.1) at `Z=0` gives the weaker `eta(0) G(0)^2 = 0`; I re-derived
   293	that independently by evaluating the raw `E3` at `Z=0`, where the coefficient of
   294	`eta(0)^3 G(0)` cancels between `-2D1E1`, `6D1E2'` and `-4D2'E1` (using
   295	`4bd = 6a`) leaving exactly `-2d eta(0)G(0)^2 = 0`. The two routes agree; (3.3)
   296	is the sharper statement.
   297	
   298	**(L3) Divisor law.** At any `z != 0` with `eta(z)G(z) = 0`,
   299	`ord_z((Z eta^2 G^2)') = 2 ord_z(eta) + 2 ord_z(G) - 1`, so (3.1) gives
   300	
   301	```text
   302	ord_z(Xi) = ord_z(eta) + 2 ord_z(G) - 1.
   303	```
   304	
   305	Summing over the `N` distinct nonzero roots of `eta G` together with (3.3)
   306	accounts for `e + 2g - 2 - N` of the `e+2g-2` zeros of `Xi`: `Xi` has exactly `N`
   307	further zeros. This is a rigidity statement, not used below, but it is the exact
   308	finite datum a later exact-computation pass should carry.
   309	
   310	## 4. THEOREM T3 — the section `s=0` is empty
   311	
   312	> **THEOREM T3.** There is no live solution (`G != 0`) with `s = 0`.
   313	
   314	*Proof.* Assume `s=0`. Then `C2 = d Z eta s = 0`, `Phi = 0`, and T1 gives
   315	`C1 = 0`. `O1` collapses to `-8 p' E2 = 0` with `E2 = bZeta^2 != 0`, so `p'=0`;
   316	`O0` becomes vacuous. `E0` reads `2 q r' = kappa != 0`, so `q = q0` and `r' = rho`
   317	are nonzero constants, `deg r = 1`, `q' = 0`, `kappa = 2 q0 rho`. Equation (1.1)
   318	becomes
   319	
   320	```text
   321	3 rho D1 + q0 E1' + kappa Z = 0,                                       (4.1)
   322	```
   323	
   324	so `deg D1 <= max(deg E1 - 1, 1)`.
   325	
   326	**Corner `e=0`.** Families (i),(ii),(iii),(v) of `Xi` all vanish (`eta'=0`,
   327	`s=0`, `q'=0`), leaving `Xi = 12a rho eta_0^3`, a nonzero constant. Law (L1)
   328	gives `0 = e+2g-2 = 2g-2`, so `g=1`; law (L2) with `epsilon=0` gives `nu>=1`, so
   329	`G = G_1 Z` exactly. Writing `B=b eta_0^2`, `A0=a eta_0^3`, `t=d eta_0` and using
   330	`6A0 = 4tB`, equation `E2eq` reduces to
   331	
   332	```text
   333	18 A0 rho Z + 2 q0 B + 2t G G' + 8t rho Z G + 4 q0 Z G' = 0,
   334	```
   335	
   336	whose `Z^2` coefficient is `8 t rho G_1 != 0`. Contradiction.
   337	
   338	**`e >= 1`.** Here `D1 = a eta^2 phi + d eta G` has candidate degrees `3e` and
   339	`e+g` with leaders `a(1+3e)eta_e^3` and `d eta_e G_g`; `E1 = b eta chi + G` has
   340	candidate degrees `2e` and `g` with leaders `b(1+2e) eta_e^2` and `G_g`.
   341	
   342	*If `g > 2e`*: `deg D1 = e+g`, `deg E1 = g`, and (4.1) forces
   343	`e+g <= max(g-1,1)`, false for `e>=1`. *If `g < 2e`*: `deg D1 = 3e`,
   344	`deg E1 = 2e`, and (4.1) forces `3e <= max(2e-1,1)`, false for `e>=1`.
   345	
   346	*If `g = 2e`*: the two leaders can drop, but not both, since
   347	`G_{2e} = -(2b/3)(1+3e)eta_e^2` (call it **Wall A**, killing `deg D1 = 3e`) and
   348	`G_{2e} = -b(1+2e)eta_e^2` (**Wall B**, killing `deg E1 = 2e`) would force
   349	`(2/3)(1+3e) = 1+2e`, i.e. `2/3 = 1`. With neither wall, (4.1) gives
   350	`3e <= 2e-1`: false. Under Wall B, `deg D1 = 3e <= max(2e-2,1)`: false. So Wall A
   351	holds, and then `deg E1 = 2e` with leader `b eta_e^2/3`.
   352	
   353	Now apply (L1) with `g=2e`, so the target degree is `5e-2`. Families present:
   354	(i) at `5e-2`, (v) at `5e-2`, (iii) at `2e-1` (with `m=0`), (iv) at `3e`
   355	(with `n=1`). For `e >= 2` we have `5e-2 > 3e` and `5e-2 > 2e-1`, so (3.2) reads
   356	
   357	```text
   358	12a e(2e-1) eta_e^3 [ b e eta_e^2 + G_{2e} ] = (3a/b)(1+6e) eta_e G_{2e}^2.
   359	```
   360	
   361	Substituting Wall A, the left side is `-4ab e(2e-1)(3e+2) eta_e^5` and the right
   362	is `(4ab/3)(1+6e)(1+3e)^2 eta_e^5`, so
   363	
   364	```text
   365	-3e(2e-1)(3e+2) = (1+6e)(1+3e)^2,  i.e.  72e^3 + 48e^2 + 6e + 1 = 0,
   366	```
   367	
   368	impossible for `e >= 1`. Only `e=1` remains, where `3e = 5e-2` and family (iv)
   369	also reaches the top.
   370	
   371	**Finish `e=1, g=2`, Wall A.** Write `eta = uZ+w` (`u != 0`),
   372	`G = G_2Z^2+G_1Z+G_0`, `G_2 = -(8b/3)u^2`, `kappa_1 := bu^2+rho`,
   373	`Lambda := kappa_1+G_2`. Then
   374	
   375	```text
   376	Xi = 12a[ kappa_1 eta^3 + u eta P2 ] + 8b q0 u eta,
   377	P2 = eta G' - uG = u G_2 Z^2 + 2w G_2 Z + (w G_1 - u G_0),
   378	```
   379	
   380	with coefficients `Xi_3 = 12a u^3 Lambda`, `Xi_2 = 36a u^2 w Lambda`,
   381	`Xi_1 = 12au[3w^2 kappa_1 + uwG_1 + 2w^2G_2 - u^2G_0] + 8b q0 u^2`.
   382	
   383	1. (4.1) forces `deg D1 <= 1`. Its `Z^3` coefficient vanishes by Wall A; its
   384	   `Z^2` coefficient is `9au^2w + d(uG_1+wG_2)`, and `dG_2 = -4au^2`, so
   385	   `5au^2w + duG_1 = 0`, i.e. `G_1 = -(10b/3)uw`.
   386	2. The right side of (3.1) is `(3a/b)[(3uZ+w)G^2 + 2Z eta G G']`. Divisibility by
   387	   `Z^2` forces its `Z^0` and `Z^1` coefficients `wG_0^2` and
   388	   `3uG_0^2+4wG_0G_1` to vanish, hence `G_0 = 0` (if `G_0!=0` then `w=0` and then
   389	   `3uG_0^2=0`).
   390	3. With `G_0=0`, the right side has `Z^5..Z^2` coefficients
   391	   `7uG_2^2`, `12uG_1G_2+5wG_2^2`, `5uG_1^2+8wG_1G_2`, `3wG_1^2` times `(3a/b)`.
   392	   Matching `Z^5` gives `Lambda = (112/9)bu^2`, i.e. `rho = (127/9)bu^2`. Matching
   393	   `Z^4` gives `448 ab u^4 w = (1280/3) ab u^4 w`, hence `w = 0`.
   394	4. `w=0` forces `G_1 = 0`, so `G = G_2Z^2` and `eta = uZ`. The right side is then
   395	   `(3a/b)7uG_2^2 Z^5` alone, so its `Z^3` coefficient is `0`, while
   396	   `Xi_1 = 8b q0 u^2`. Hence `q0 = 0`, contradicting `q0 != 0`. QED
   397	
   398	Consequently every live pair has `s != 0`, so `C2 != 0` and, by T1,
   399	`Phi = sG/eta` is a nonzero polynomial. The charged spec's `s=0` sub-branch
   400	(`spec:228-236`) was established only inside `F=G=0`; T3 is its live-section
   401	counterpart and does not follow from it.
   402	
   403	## 5. THEOREM T4 — the section `e=0` is empty
   404	
   405	> **THEOREM T4.** There is no live solution with `deg eta = 0`.
   406	
   407	Put `eta = eta_0 != 0`, `B := b eta_0^2`, `A0 := a eta_0^3`, `t := d eta_0`,
   408	`mu := t/(2B)`. The scalar identities used below are
   409	
   410	```text
   411	B t = (3/2) A0,      A0/B = (2/3) t,      t^2/A0 = 3 mu.
   412	```
   413	
   414	By T3, `s != 0`. T1 gives `C1 = t s + mu s G` (the divisibility is vacuous at
   415	`e=0`). Set `I := 3A0 r' - 2B q' + t s s'`.
   416	
   417	**5.1 The three working equations.** Substituting into `E3eq`, `E1eq` (in the
   418	form (1.1)) and `E2eq` and using `6A0 = 4tB`:
   419	
   420	```text
   421	E3d:  2 Z^2 I = t (Z G^2)'.
   422	E1d:  I + 3tGr' - 2q'G + qG' + mu s(s'G - sG') + kappa Z = 0.
   423	E2d:  6ZI + 2Bq + 8tZGr' - 4Zq'G + 4ZqG' + 2tGG' - 2 mu s^2 L(G) = 0,
   424	      L(G) := 2ZG' - G.
   425	K1 := (E2d - 6Z*E1d)/2:
   426	      Bq - 5tZGr' + 4Zq'G - ZqG' + tGG' + mu s^2 G + mu Z s^2 G'
   427	      - 3 mu Z s s' G - 3 kappa Z^2 = 0.
   428	```
   429	
   430	`E3d` is (3.1) at `e=0` (there `Xi = 4I`), and I cross-checked it against the
   431	spec's `e=0` display `4Z^2 I = 0` (`spec:220-226`), which is the `G=0`
   432	specialization. From `E3d`, `ord_Z(G^2+2ZGG') = 2 nu` exactly, so `nu >= 1`
   433	(this is (L2) with `epsilon = 0`), and
   434	
   435	```text
   436	deg I = 2g-2,    leader(I) = t(1+2g) G_g^2 / 2,    g >= 1.            (5.1)
   437	```
   438	
   439	Evaluating `E2d` at `Z=0` (all terms carry `Z`, `G` or `L(G)`, and
   440	`G(0)=L(G)(0)=0`) gives `2Bq(0)=0`, so `Z | q`. Evaluating `E0` at `Z=0` then
   441	gives `-2p'(0)s(0) = kappa != 0`, so `s(0) != 0`. (A further `[Z^1]` expansion of
   442	`E1d` gives `nu <= 2`, but the proof below does not need it.)
   443	
   444	**5.2 The three top-degree relations.** Write `Mx := max(m, n, 2 sigma)`, with
   445	the `n`-family absent when `r'=0` and the `m`-family absent when `q=0`; the
   446	`sigma`-family always carries a factor `sigma`, so `sigma=0` contributes nothing
   447	to `I`. Since `deg I <= Mx - 1`, (5.1) forces
   448	
   449	```text
   450	Mx >= 2g - 1.                                                          (5.2)
   451	```
   452	
   453	Comparing degrees inside `E1d` and `K1` — the `I` family sits at `2g-2`, the
   454	`tGG'` family at `2g-1`, the `kappa` families at degree `1` resp. `2`, and
   455	`Bq` at `m < m+g` — the strict maxima are `g-1+Mx` in `E1d` and `g+Mx` in `K1`
   456	whenever `Mx >= 2`, because `Mx >= 2g-1 > g-1`. Reading off the three top
   457	coefficients (and dividing `E1d`,`K1` by `G_g`):
   458	
   459	```text
   460	(E3top) [n=Mx] 3A0 n r_n - [m=Mx] 2B m q_m + [2sigma=Mx] t sigma s_sigma^2
   461	        =  t(1+2g)G_g^2/2   if Mx = 2g-1,      =  0   if Mx >= 2g;
   462	(E1top) [n=Mx] 3t n r_n + [m=Mx] (g-2m) q_m + [2sigma=Mx] mu s_sigma^2(sigma-g) = 0;
   463	(K1top) [m=Mx] (4m-g) q_m - [n=Mx] 5t n r_n + [2sigma=Mx] mu s_sigma^2(1+g-3sigma) = 0.
   464	```
   465	
   466	**5.3 `Mx = 2g-1`.** This is odd, so `2sigma != Mx`; only `m` and/or `n` are
   467	active. If only `n`: `(K1top)` gives `-5t(2g-1) r_n = 0`, so `r_n = 0`, false.
   468	If only `m`: `(K1top)` gives `(4m-g)q_m = 0` with `q_m != 0` (forced by
   469	`(E3top)`, whose right side is nonzero), so `4(2g-1) = g`, i.e. `7g = 4`: not an
   470	integer. If both, `(E1top)` gives `(2Mx-g)q_m = 3tMx r_n`, i.e.
   471	`q_m = 3t(2g-1)r_n/(3g-2)`, and `(K1top)` gives `(7g-4)q_m = 5t(2g-1)r_n`;
   472	eliminating `q_m` (legitimate: `r_n != 0`, `2g-1 != 0`, `3g-2 != 0`) yields
   473	`3(7g-4) = 5(3g-2)`, i.e. `6g = 2`, `g = 1/3`. Not an integer. All three
   474	sub-cases die.
   475	
   476	**5.4 `Mx >= 2g`.** Now `(E3top)` is homogeneous, and the three relations are a
   477	homogeneous linear system in the leaders. Singles: `n` alone gives
   478	`3A0 Mx r_n = 0`; `m` alone gives `2B Mx q_m = 0`; `2sigma` alone gives
   479	`t sigma s_sigma^2 = 0` with `sigma = Mx/2 >= g >= 1`. All false. Pairs:
   480	
   481	* `{m,n}`: `(E3top)` gives `3A0 r_n = 2B q_m`, i.e. `q_m = t r_n`; then
   482	  `(K1top)` gives `4Mx - g = 5Mx`, i.e. `Mx = -g`. False.
   483	* `{m,2sigma}` (`sigma = Mx/2`): `(E3top)` gives `q_m = mu s_sigma^2/2`; then
   484	  `(K1top)` gives `(4Mx-g)/2 + 1 + g - 3Mx/2 = 0`, i.e. `Mx/2 + g/2 + 1 = 0`.
   485	  False.
   486	* `{n,2sigma}`: `(E3top)` gives `3A0 r_n = -t s_sigma^2/2`; then `(K1top)`, using
   487	  `t^2/A0 = 3mu`, gives `mu(Mx + 1 + g) = 0`. False.
   488	
   489	All three: set `X = t r_n`, `Y = q_m`, `W = mu s_sigma^2`, `M = Mx`,
   490	`sigma = M/2`. Dividing `(E3top)` by `MB` and using `A0/B = (2/3)t`,
   491	`mu = t/(2B)`, the system is
   492	
   493	```text
   494	   2 X  -   2 Y   +          W        = 0
   495	 3M X  + (g-2M) Y + (M/2 - g) W       = 0
   496	-5M X  + (4M-g) Y + (1+g-3M/2) W      = 0
   497	```
   498	
   499	with determinant `2[(g+gM-2M+M^2)] + 2[(3M-2gM-2M^2)] + [2M^2+2gM] = 2(g+M)`,
   500	which is nonzero because `g >= 1` and `M >= 2`. Hence `X=Y=W=0`, contradicting
   501	`r_n != 0`. Every `Mx >= 2g` configuration dies.
   502	
   503	**5.5 The residue `Mx = 1`.** By (5.2) this occurs only for `g = 1`, and then
   504	`1 <= nu <= g` forces `G = G_1 Z`, `sigma = 0`, `m <= 1`, `n <= 1`; with `Z|q`,
   505	`q = q_1 Z` and `r = r_0 + r_1 Z`, `s = s_0 != 0`. `E3d` gives
   506	`I = (3t/2)G_1^2`. `E0` gives `2 s_0 p' = 2q_1 r_1 Z - kappa`. Substituting into
   507	`O0` and clearing `2s_0`:
   508	
   509	```text
   510	8t s_0^2 r_1 + 4t s_0^2 r_1 G_1 Z/B - 4(B+G_1Z)(2q_1r_1Z - kappa) - 4q_1 s_0^2 = 0.
   511	```
   512	
   513	Its `Z^2` coefficient is `-8 q_1 r_1 G_1`, so `q_1 r_1 = 0`. If `r_1 = 0` the
   514	`Z^1` coefficient reduces to `4 kappa G_1 = 0`: false. If `q_1 = 0` the `Z^1` and
   515	`Z^0` coefficients give `kappa = -t s_0^2 r_1/B` and `kappa = -2t s_0^2 r_1/B`,
   516	forcing `r_1 = 0` and `kappa = 0`: false.
   517	
   518	Sections 5.3–5.5 exhaust `Mx`, so `e = 0` is empty. QED
   519	
   520	T4 is also what removes `e=0` from the `r'=0` analysis of Theorem T5 below.

